WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.324 --> 00:00:02.740
JEFF DAVIS: Welcome to the
Google Cloud Platform Education

00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:03.760
series.

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:05.350
My name is Jeff Davis.

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:06.820
And in this session
we'll discuss

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:09.490
creating best in class
storage solutions

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:11.890
with Google Cloud Storage.

00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:15.470
We'll be doing a moderated
Q&amp;A throughout the session,

00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:18.910
so feel free to present
your questions in the Ask

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:20.740
Your Question Here field.

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:22.280
Let's get started.

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:24.190
So this is our agenda
for the session.

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:26.900
We're going to begin
with a series of demos.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:30.190
These are going to highlight the
most typical use cases and just

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:34.060
how easy it is to set up
high-performing, really

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:38.100
functional solutions on top
of Google Cloud Storage.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:40.470
Out of the demos, we'll
move into a discussion

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:43.140
of the overall GCS
feature set and how

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:46.380
it might apply to challenges
that you're tackling

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:48.412
within your organization.

00:00:48.412 --> 00:00:50.370
We'll take a few minutes
to talk about the best

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.950
ways to find information
after this session

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:55.440
if you have questions.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:58.320
And we'll also talk
about how to evaluate

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:01.714
GCS for specific use
cases, specific problems

00:01:01.714 --> 00:01:02.880
that you're trying to solve.

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:04.296
And again, throughout
the session,

00:01:04.296 --> 00:01:07.170
feel free to submit
your questions.

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:08.955
So on to the demos.

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:17.150
The first use case that
is very typical with GCS

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:21.200
is using it as a target
for offsite backups.

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.680
So I'm going to bring up a
piece of backup software.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:27.170
This is a product
called Retrospect.

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:29.330
There are lots of different
products in this class.

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:33.200
Veritas, for instance,
is a partner with Google

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.300
and has their
NetBackup solution.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:39.200
Lots of solutions will actually
take your onsite backup data

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.360
and pump it into
the Cloud, where

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:44.420
you have then an offsite
backup and it can be stored--

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:48.434
large amounts of data for
very small amounts of dollars.

00:01:48.434 --> 00:01:49.850
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:51.860
going to start by
configuring the product

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:55.890
so that it will use Google Cloud
Storage as its target location.

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:57.980
So I'm going to
create a mediaset.

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:00.470
And you'll notice that I
have a variety of options.

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:03.530
I'm going to choose to
send it to the Cloud.

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:07.670
We're going to call
this Demo Backup Set.

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:11.660
And we'll go ahead and
encrypt it and give it

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:12.850
a simple password.

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:21.710
Now at this point I'm
prompted for what backend

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:24.260
cloud service I want to use.

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:28.970
And I need to use a
configuration string provided

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:32.870
to me by Google.

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:36.995
So that is Storage API's
GCP webinar backups.

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.340
And then I need an access
key and a secret key.

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:46.080
And this is easily configured
within the Google Cloud Storage

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:47.050
user interface.

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:50.670
So I'm going to actually
go to Google Cloud Storage.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.020
This is my project.

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:53.520
I've got a set of buckets.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:57.480
You'll notice my GCP webinar
backups bucket is here.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:01.440
I'm going to click on
Settings, Interoperability.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.120
And you'll notice that I
already have some keys created,

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:06.450
so I'm going to actually
use one of these keys.

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:11.920
So I need the access key,
and that's going to go here.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:17.135
And then I need the secret key,
and that's going to go here.

00:03:17.135 --> 00:03:19.560
It uses SSL to
transfer the data.

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:22.710
So it's encrypted in-flight.

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:24.247
And now I have my backup set.

00:03:24.247 --> 00:03:25.830
Now I'm going to
actually do a backup.

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:28.230
Retrospect can backup an
individual machine, which

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:29.520
is what I'm going to do here.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:32.640
It can back up all the
workstations on a network

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:34.900
or it can back up multiple
servers on a network.

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:38.130
So this could be backups
of your internal servers

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:40.950
that are application servers
or file servers, as well as

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:44.220
backups of all of your
important workstation data.

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:47.820
So I'm using this simply
because it's easy to install

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:51.240
and it's relatively
quick to demonstrate.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:54.540
So I'm going to select
what I want to back up.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:57.170
And I don't want to back
up my entire hard drive

00:03:57.170 --> 00:03:59.520
because we'll be here too long.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:04.560
Instead what I want to do is I
want to pick a videos folder.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:06.840
And I back that folder up.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:09.210
And I'm going to back
it up to the Cloud.

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:11.850
Notice it's a cloud
demo backup set.

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:13.650
And I'm going to run it.

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:17.950
Now, it takes a little while
for this backup job to execute,

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:21.510
so I'm going to go ahead and
let that run in the background

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:26.500
while we move on to the
next part of the demo.

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:28.030
Let's take a look
at performance.

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:30.790
One of the things that
you care about not only

00:04:30.790 --> 00:04:34.390
is storing large amounts
of data inexpensively,

00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:36.940
but you want to make sure
that your access to that data

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:40.210
is fast and is also reliable.

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:45.950
So again, if I go to my bucket
browser for this project,

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:48.460
you'll notice that I've
got a variety of buckets

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:50.470
other than my backups bucket.

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:56.560
So I have a regional bucket in
the west of the United States,

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:58.660
I have a multi-regional
bucket, and then

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:01.046
I have a Nearline bucket
and a Coldline bucket.

00:05:01.046 --> 00:05:02.920
And these are four
different storage classes.

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:04.600
And I'm going to show
you how performance looks

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:06.141
for each one of
these, and then we'll

00:05:06.141 --> 00:05:09.160
talk about the differences
in a little bit.

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:13.960
So in order to test performance
across a variety of areas,

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:18.080
I've actually created a
bunch of test instances.

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:20.770
So you'll notice that I've
got virtual machines running

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:22.790
in different parts of the world.

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:24.730
So I've got one in
Asia, I've got one

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:27.400
on the East Coast which
is not in the same region

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:29.036
as my regional
bucket, I've got one

00:05:29.036 --> 00:05:30.910
on the West Coast which
is in the same region

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:33.940
as my regional bucket, and
I've got one in Europe.

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:35.440
So what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:42.880
to SSH into each of
these virtual machines

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:50.180
so that I can generate
load against GCS.

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:54.320
So let me shrink
that window down.

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:57.150
Notice my backup is still
running in the background,

00:05:57.150 --> 00:05:59.240
so I'm going to shrink
that down as well.

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:03.930
And what I have here
is four SSH windows--

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:07.120
one to each of my
different machines.

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:09.210
So I'm going to
use a cURL command.

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:15.210
cURL is a command that basically
is going to do a URL request.

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:18.910
The items that I'm
pulling out of the buckets

00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:20.860
have actually been made
publicly accessible

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:23.531
and can be accessed
using a standard URL.

00:06:23.531 --> 00:06:25.030
So what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:30.769
to copy down a relatively big
file from a regional bucket.

00:06:30.769 --> 00:06:32.560
So this bucket is stored
in the West Coast.

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:34.780
And what we really want to
see is, is it fast, man?

00:06:34.780 --> 00:06:38.270
Can I pull down big
files really quickly?

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:42.250
So I'm going to go ahead
and enter this command

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:44.650
in each of the windows.

00:06:47.270 --> 00:06:49.467
And what this should do
is start a file download

00:06:49.467 --> 00:06:51.800
from four different instances
in four different regions.

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:55.640
And what we want to see is, what
does the performance look like?

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:58.040
So if I look at the
instance in the West,

00:06:58.040 --> 00:07:04.490
you can see I'm averaging 292
megabytes per second download.

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:06.740
That's pretty quick.

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:08.840
If I look in the East
of the United States,

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:12.290
it's about 314 megabytes-- so
roughly the same performance.

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:15.710
If I look in EMEA,
it's 203 megabytes.

00:07:15.710 --> 00:07:19.460
And this is because the client
that's running in Europe

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:24.710
is accessing resources stored
in a Western region bucket.

00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:26.480
That means that it
is not connected

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:30.830
with the same high throughput
low latency connections

00:07:30.830 --> 00:07:33.620
that, say, this instance
in the West Coast is.

00:07:33.620 --> 00:07:35.330
So relatively quick.

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:37.160
We averaged-- it looks like--

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:40.850
96 megabytes per
second in Europe.

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:44.090
We averaged 85 megabytes per
second in the East Coast.

00:07:44.090 --> 00:07:46.420
We managed about 100
megabytes per second.

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:48.740
And in Asia, about 92.

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:52.400
So pretty good performance
around the globe from data

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:56.231
that's stored in the Western
part of the United States.

00:07:56.231 --> 00:07:57.980
I'm going to do the
same thing, except I'm

00:07:57.980 --> 00:08:01.130
going to copy this not from
my Western regional bucket,

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:03.440
but instead from a
multi-regional bucket.

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:06.080
So a multi-regional
bucket is a bucket

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:08.480
where your data is
actually replicated,

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:11.540
not just in the Western United
States, but in the Central

00:08:11.540 --> 00:08:13.964
and in the Eastern
United States as well.

00:08:13.964 --> 00:08:15.380
What this means
is that it's going

00:08:15.380 --> 00:08:19.880
to be closer to a
wider range of clients.

00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:21.330
So again, I'm
going to copy this.

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:23.570
And we're going to
start it in Asia

00:08:23.570 --> 00:08:25.377
and then we're going
to go to Europe

00:08:25.377 --> 00:08:27.710
and then we're going to go
to the Western United States.

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:29.043
And there was one more in there.

00:08:29.043 --> 00:08:31.130
There's the East.

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:33.830
And you can see that the
multi-regional bucket

00:08:33.830 --> 00:08:37.909
is even faster.

00:08:37.909 --> 00:08:40.850
So again, we're at
roughly a gigabyte

00:08:40.850 --> 00:08:43.471
per second in the West.

00:08:43.471 --> 00:08:46.830
In EMEA, we're at
about 500 megabytes.

00:08:46.830 --> 00:08:50.010
In the East, we're
at about a gigabyte.

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:53.100
And in Asia Pacific,
we're at about 413.

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:55.920
So again, multi-regional
buckets are even faster

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:58.770
than the regional buckets.

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:01.070
Now I'm going to do
a small file from

00:09:01.070 --> 00:09:02.630
the multi-regional buckets.

00:09:02.630 --> 00:09:04.710
And this is important
for the next test,

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:05.900
which is the Nearline test.

00:09:05.900 --> 00:09:07.441
So what I want to
do is I want to see

00:09:07.441 --> 00:09:10.100
if you download this small
file, how fast is it?

00:09:10.100 --> 00:09:12.740
And again, super fast.

00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:14.830
Super fast.

00:09:14.830 --> 00:09:16.600
Super fast.

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:18.640
Super fast.

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:21.340
Now, Nearline, as you may know--

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:24.080
Nearline is a less
expensive storage class

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:27.490
that's intended for data that
isn't accessed that frequently.

00:09:27.490 --> 00:09:31.180
So they move it a
little bit further away

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:32.560
from the standard access points.

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:36.130
It means that you
can access it, but it

00:09:36.130 --> 00:09:38.234
is going to cost you
every time you read it.

00:09:38.234 --> 00:09:40.150
We'll talk about the
pricing in just a second.

00:09:40.150 --> 00:09:42.940
But what I want to show you here
is even though you've moved it

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:48.850
to a tiered storage
offering, it doesn't actually

00:09:48.850 --> 00:09:54.100
slow down the speed with which
you can access or download

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:55.000
the files.

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:56.270
So here we go.

00:09:56.270 --> 00:10:00.490
You'll notice from Nearline,
I'm still seeing 652 megabytes.

00:10:00.490 --> 00:10:02.740
I'm seeing 440 megabytes.

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:05.350
I'm seeing 761 megabytes.

00:10:05.350 --> 00:10:07.840
I'm seeing 380 megabytes.

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:11.380
Notice access time was tens
or hundreds of milliseconds.

00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:12.670
It was really quick.

00:10:12.670 --> 00:10:14.560
And it was the same
command to access it

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:18.250
from Nearline as from a regional
or multi-regional bucket.

00:10:18.250 --> 00:10:19.880
Now let's go a step further.

00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:22.150
Let's go to Coldline.

00:10:22.150 --> 00:10:24.520
So Coldline is even
less expensive.

00:10:24.520 --> 00:10:28.360
It's intended for data that you
only access very infrequently.

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:32.020
But again, use the same
access mechanisms and you

00:10:32.020 --> 00:10:36.520
get the same retrieval speeds
as you do from standard buckets.

00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:46.060
So you'll notice
413, 778, 511, 929.

00:10:46.060 --> 00:10:49.240
So even though you've
moved to tiered storage

00:10:49.240 --> 00:10:52.930
and you've gone from regional
to Nearline to Coldline storage,

00:10:52.930 --> 00:10:56.860
you haven't had to sacrifice
initial access time or download

00:10:56.860 --> 00:11:00.010
throughput or change
the APIs that you use.

00:11:00.010 --> 00:11:03.850
This is a big plus relative
to some other solutions

00:11:03.850 --> 00:11:05.500
that are available to you.

00:11:05.500 --> 00:11:10.750
In the meantime, let me go back
and check the backup software.

00:11:10.750 --> 00:11:13.840
You can see that the
backup is actually running.

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:16.529
We'll take a look when it's
done to see that it's actually

00:11:16.529 --> 00:11:17.820
stored the stuff in the bucket.

00:11:17.820 --> 00:11:20.170
So we've got backup
running, we've

00:11:20.170 --> 00:11:23.170
just showed performance and
the relative performance

00:11:23.170 --> 00:11:25.810
of a multi-regional bucket
versus a regional bucket

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:28.600
versus Nearline versus Coldline.

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:32.284
So now let's take a look
at a couple of other things

00:11:32.284 --> 00:11:32.950
that you can do.

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:41.610
So one frequent use of
Google Cloud Storage

00:11:41.610 --> 00:11:45.930
is to store large media objects
for serving on websites.

00:11:45.930 --> 00:11:49.800
So here we have an example
learning management system

00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:54.360
that's got videos embedded in
their micro learning lessons.

00:11:54.360 --> 00:11:57.450
And you can see this module
is all about the fact

00:11:57.450 --> 00:11:58.520
that mantas are awesome.

00:11:58.520 --> 00:12:01.320
This is a video that
I took while diving

00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:03.570
with mantas off of Kona.

00:12:03.570 --> 00:12:08.610
If I load this page, you can see
that it actually is streaming

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:11.380
the video to my machine.

00:12:11.380 --> 00:12:15.060
Now, this video is stored
in Google Cloud Storage.

00:12:15.060 --> 00:12:17.960
And in fact, if we go
to the Cloud Storage

00:12:17.960 --> 00:12:22.410
UI, what you can see is here
is a bucket for that project.

00:12:22.410 --> 00:12:25.170
And this bucket has
got a couple of copies

00:12:25.170 --> 00:12:28.619
of the mantas
streaming video file.

00:12:28.619 --> 00:12:30.160
It has a bunch of
other ones as well.

00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:32.520
So what happens is you
set up your web server

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:34.680
and it simply proxies
these media files.

00:12:34.680 --> 00:12:37.770
You can see that these
files are shared publicly

00:12:37.770 --> 00:12:39.750
and there's a
public link option.

00:12:39.750 --> 00:12:43.080
If you click on the public link,
this actually gives you a URL

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:46.770
and you can serve the
video directly from GCS.

00:12:46.770 --> 00:12:50.230
There's me talking
about something else.

00:12:50.230 --> 00:12:53.530
So this is a very
common application,

00:12:53.530 --> 00:12:54.960
and it's super, super easy.

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:57.850
You just upload your files,
you click on Share Publicly,

00:12:57.850 --> 00:12:59.350
and you get a link
that you can then

00:12:59.350 --> 00:13:02.230
publish within your web pages.

00:13:02.230 --> 00:13:04.465
So let me close
those two windows.

00:13:04.465 --> 00:13:08.155
I'm going to check on my
backup and see how we're doing.

00:13:08.155 --> 00:13:10.290
It's still thinking about it.

00:13:10.290 --> 00:13:11.670
So I'm going to
let that continue

00:13:11.670 --> 00:13:14.130
to bake in the background.

00:13:14.130 --> 00:13:19.400
It's going to go into the
webinar backups bucket.

00:13:19.400 --> 00:13:22.990
And you can see that there
is a demo backup set.

00:13:22.990 --> 00:13:25.850
And this is where the
backup is occurring.

00:13:25.850 --> 00:13:30.310
It's not done yet, but
it's backing stuff up.

00:13:30.310 --> 00:13:31.930
Let's look at
another application.

00:13:31.930 --> 00:13:36.120
So GCS is a really good
place to stage data

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:41.530
that you are moving into or
out of data analytics jobs.

00:13:41.530 --> 00:13:45.460
So here's an example of
a big analytics table

00:13:45.460 --> 00:13:49.900
that I use for a customer
success application

00:13:49.900 --> 00:13:51.070
that I've built.

00:13:51.070 --> 00:13:54.820
So you can see that this
is a BigQuery table.

00:13:54.820 --> 00:13:57.040
It It's not that big.

00:13:57.040 --> 00:14:00.130
It has 525,000 rows.

00:14:00.130 --> 00:14:02.050
There are obviously
much bigger ones

00:14:02.050 --> 00:14:03.880
if you look at the
BigQuery samples.

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:06.040
You can go to the
Wikipedia benchmark

00:14:06.040 --> 00:14:09.220
and you'll see that they've
got one million rows, one

00:14:09.220 --> 00:14:12.520
billion rows, 100 billion rows.

00:14:12.520 --> 00:14:16.400
So that makes my little
dataset look paltry actually.

00:14:16.400 --> 00:14:21.787
But 525,000 rows would be
a lot to write row by row.

00:14:21.787 --> 00:14:23.620
So instead what we want
to do is we actually

00:14:23.620 --> 00:14:25.600
want to import this data.

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:29.170
And the easiest way to import
it is you upload it to GCS,

00:14:29.170 --> 00:14:33.646
and then GCS has a really easy
integration with BigQuery.

00:14:33.646 --> 00:14:36.910
So if you look, here's a bucket.

00:14:36.910 --> 00:14:40.720
This is the bucket associated
with the success HQ software.

00:14:40.720 --> 00:14:44.440
That's the customer
success platform.

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:49.090
Again, it's not a huge file,
but it's a 48 megabyte file.

00:14:49.090 --> 00:14:53.440
And this has got
the 525,000 rows.

00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:55.730
Think of it as being
500 million rows.

00:14:55.730 --> 00:14:58.510
Again, you just upload
it to the bucket.

00:14:58.510 --> 00:15:01.630
And then in BigQuery, if
you want to import it,

00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:03.570
you simply say, I want
to create a new table.

00:15:06.460 --> 00:15:10.769
And you say, I want
to choose a file.

00:15:10.769 --> 00:15:12.310
Actually, I don't
want a file upload.

00:15:12.310 --> 00:15:15.820
I want to do Google
Cloud Storage.

00:15:15.820 --> 00:15:21.750
And then you need
the path to the file.

00:15:21.750 --> 00:15:26.300
So that's going to be in the--

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:27.050
let's see.

00:15:27.050 --> 00:15:28.550
Yes.

00:15:28.550 --> 00:15:33.840
GS colon slash slash
shq transfer slash--

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:38.770
and then the name
of the file is this.

00:15:38.770 --> 00:15:41.352
And then I could specify
the schema explicitly.

00:15:41.352 --> 00:15:42.810
I'm going to actually
have BigQuery

00:15:42.810 --> 00:15:44.980
try to automatically detect it.

00:15:44.980 --> 00:15:50.399
And I'm going to give it
a demo events table name

00:15:50.399 --> 00:15:51.190
and create a table.

00:15:51.190 --> 00:15:53.740
And this then inserts
a job, and it's

00:15:53.740 --> 00:15:58.060
going to import the CSV file
from Google Cloud Storage.

00:15:58.060 --> 00:16:00.670
It'll take-- if I'm not lying--

00:16:00.670 --> 00:16:02.590
about five more seconds.

00:16:02.590 --> 00:16:03.550
And it should be done.

00:16:03.550 --> 00:16:06.280
You'll notice here we've got the
entries showing that the demo

00:16:06.280 --> 00:16:08.650
events table is loading.

00:16:08.650 --> 00:16:09.610
Oh, there we go.

00:16:09.610 --> 00:16:10.630
We're done.

00:16:10.630 --> 00:16:14.050
So you'll notice it's
created fields for each one.

00:16:14.050 --> 00:16:16.150
In Details, you
can see that we've

00:16:16.150 --> 00:16:20.050
imported 522,000 some odd rows.

00:16:20.050 --> 00:16:22.420
And if I want to see
that this data is,

00:16:22.420 --> 00:16:27.470
in fact, live, what
I can do is I can--

00:16:27.470 --> 00:16:31.330
let's see, shrink this down
a little bit, pick this,

00:16:31.330 --> 00:16:41.535
and I'm going to do Select star
from events.demo events table.

00:16:47.602 --> 00:16:48.560
So let's run the query.

00:16:51.400 --> 00:16:55.720
And if all goes well, we should
see I get all of the event data

00:16:55.720 --> 00:16:58.930
that's recorded by my
customer success system.

00:16:58.930 --> 00:17:02.920
And I have 522,000 rows.

00:17:02.920 --> 00:17:06.339
So again, that took
about 10 to 15 seconds.

00:17:06.339 --> 00:17:10.060
If you extrapolate that--
so if you go to 500 million,

00:17:10.060 --> 00:17:12.760
it's still a relatively
quick import.

00:17:12.760 --> 00:17:16.359
And again, this is using
GCS as a staging mechanism

00:17:16.359 --> 00:17:20.319
for getting data into
BigQuery or into Dataflow

00:17:20.319 --> 00:17:21.790
or into Dataproc.

00:17:21.790 --> 00:17:26.030
It's also a great target
repository for GPC services.

00:17:26.030 --> 00:17:28.450
So any of those big
data analytics tools

00:17:28.450 --> 00:17:31.510
can actually dump the data
into Google Cloud Storage,

00:17:31.510 --> 00:17:35.230
and then you can actually
share this with other folks.

00:17:35.230 --> 00:17:38.710
Let's switch back to my backup.

00:17:38.710 --> 00:17:41.960
And it looks like my backup
is actually complete.

00:17:41.960 --> 00:17:45.370
So if I go to the
backup bucket, I

00:17:45.370 --> 00:17:49.390
should now have a
full demo backup set.

00:17:49.390 --> 00:17:52.730
And this was about
a gigabyte of data.

00:17:52.730 --> 00:17:56.560
So you can see there's a 600
meg file and a 420 meg file.

00:17:56.560 --> 00:17:58.900
I had about a gigabyte
worth of videos that I

00:17:58.900 --> 00:18:02.030
uploaded relatively quickly.

00:18:02.030 --> 00:18:05.320
So what we've got here
is examples of a variety

00:18:05.320 --> 00:18:06.440
of different applications.

00:18:06.440 --> 00:18:09.310
We have files that are
just stored for download.

00:18:09.310 --> 00:18:11.284
This might be
appropriate, for instance,

00:18:11.284 --> 00:18:13.450
if you're a software company
and you're distributing

00:18:13.450 --> 00:18:16.120
your application and you want
to make sure people can download

00:18:16.120 --> 00:18:19.210
the application and that
it's highly available

00:18:19.210 --> 00:18:21.010
and a good download performance.

00:18:21.010 --> 00:18:23.240
We did backups, where
we took large amounts--

00:18:23.240 --> 00:18:25.090
in this case, only a
gigabyte-- but, again,

00:18:25.090 --> 00:18:26.867
if you do this across
a bunch of machines,

00:18:26.867 --> 00:18:29.200
you would take all of that
data and you would pump it up

00:18:29.200 --> 00:18:30.220
to the Cloud.

00:18:30.220 --> 00:18:32.440
And then you could actually
store these backups

00:18:32.440 --> 00:18:34.030
in various tiers of storage.

00:18:34.030 --> 00:18:37.690
So old backups, you could
have in Coldline and so forth.

00:18:37.690 --> 00:18:41.380
And we talked about serving
media in web applications

00:18:41.380 --> 00:18:44.920
and we talked about using
GCS as a staging area

00:18:44.920 --> 00:18:50.590
for getting data in and out of
big data analytics pipelines.

00:18:50.590 --> 00:18:53.110
Hopefully you've seen at
least one or two things

00:18:53.110 --> 00:18:56.500
here that really gets
you excited and curious

00:18:56.500 --> 00:19:00.710
as to how you can leverage
GCS for your company.

00:19:00.710 --> 00:19:06.160
So let's switch back
to the presentation

00:19:06.160 --> 00:19:09.460
and let's talk at a high
level about the wide range

00:19:09.460 --> 00:19:12.130
of capabilities and
the pricing information

00:19:12.130 --> 00:19:14.900
about Cloud Storage.

00:19:14.900 --> 00:19:19.540
So again, Cloud Storage is
a unified storage product

00:19:19.540 --> 00:19:26.150
that runs atop of Google's
massive storage and networking

00:19:26.150 --> 00:19:27.630
infrastructure.

00:19:27.630 --> 00:19:31.940
It is BLOB storage, or
Binary Large Object storage.

00:19:31.940 --> 00:19:34.040
This is not a
regular file system

00:19:34.040 --> 00:19:37.910
where you're reading and writing
and updating data within files.

00:19:37.910 --> 00:19:41.900
This is where you store lots
of files that are relatively

00:19:41.900 --> 00:19:45.170
static and you read them
periodically for analysis

00:19:45.170 --> 00:19:47.510
or you just keep
them in archival.

00:19:47.510 --> 00:19:52.190
And they feed into things
like live data serving

00:19:52.190 --> 00:19:54.230
or analytics, that
sort of stuff.

00:19:54.230 --> 00:19:57.980
You have one consistent
API to access

00:19:57.980 --> 00:19:59.900
all of the different
types of storage.

00:19:59.900 --> 00:20:03.620
You have similar latency
and download speeds

00:20:03.620 --> 00:20:05.240
across those
classes, which makes

00:20:05.240 --> 00:20:07.889
it super easy to work with.

00:20:07.889 --> 00:20:09.305
So here's a slide
that we're going

00:20:09.305 --> 00:20:11.630
to spend a little time on.

00:20:11.630 --> 00:20:15.050
This details the four
different storage classes

00:20:15.050 --> 00:20:18.740
and their differences
within Google Cloud Storage.

00:20:18.740 --> 00:20:22.340
So the one that most people
start with is regional storage.

00:20:22.340 --> 00:20:24.530
And remember, in
Google Cloud Platform,

00:20:24.530 --> 00:20:27.470
a region is a
collection of resources

00:20:27.470 --> 00:20:31.070
that are interconnected with
high-throughput, low-latency

00:20:31.070 --> 00:20:32.360
network connections.

00:20:32.360 --> 00:20:34.940
That means that an
instance in the West region

00:20:34.940 --> 00:20:39.660
can read and write data to a GCS
bucket in the West region very,

00:20:39.660 --> 00:20:42.110
very quickly.

00:20:42.110 --> 00:20:46.520
Your data-- when you write it
into Google Cloud Storage--

00:20:46.520 --> 00:20:51.200
is actually replicated across
machines throughout the region.

00:20:51.200 --> 00:20:54.050
What this means is you have
very, very high durability

00:20:54.050 --> 00:20:55.940
and very high availability.

00:20:55.940 --> 00:20:58.010
The likelihood that you
are going to lose data

00:20:58.010 --> 00:21:02.120
is extremely low, because
even if a particular storage

00:21:02.120 --> 00:21:04.160
device drops out, your
data is replicated.

00:21:04.160 --> 00:21:06.590
Even if a zone
goes out, your data

00:21:06.590 --> 00:21:10.430
is replicated across other
zones within the region.

00:21:10.430 --> 00:21:12.620
Your data is also
highly available,

00:21:12.620 --> 00:21:14.960
which means that, again,
if a zone goes down,

00:21:14.960 --> 00:21:16.730
you'll have live
copies of your data

00:21:16.730 --> 00:21:18.590
in another zone
within the same region

00:21:18.590 --> 00:21:21.460
and you can access it there.

00:21:21.460 --> 00:21:23.740
Then people sort of
move to multi-regional.

00:21:23.740 --> 00:21:28.390
Multi-regional is where
you have resources

00:21:28.390 --> 00:21:30.620
that are holding your data.

00:21:30.620 --> 00:21:32.620
And they're not just
spread out within a region,

00:21:32.620 --> 00:21:34.370
they're actually spread
out through what's

00:21:34.370 --> 00:21:35.860
called a multi-regional area.

00:21:35.860 --> 00:21:37.840
So there are three
multi-regional areas

00:21:37.840 --> 00:21:39.380
in Google Cloud Platform.

00:21:39.380 --> 00:21:41.740
There's a North
American, or a US region,

00:21:41.740 --> 00:21:45.290
there's an EMEA, and
an Asia Pacific region.

00:21:45.290 --> 00:21:49.860
So this gives you even higher
levels of availability.

00:21:49.860 --> 00:21:52.640
If the Western
region goes down, you

00:21:52.640 --> 00:21:55.430
can still read your
data from the Central

00:21:55.430 --> 00:21:56.676
or the Eastern region.

00:21:56.676 --> 00:21:58.550
So you can see actually
that the availability

00:21:58.550 --> 00:22:01.760
is better with multi-regional
as opposed to regional.

00:22:01.760 --> 00:22:03.800
You also get a
performance improvement,

00:22:03.800 --> 00:22:07.940
in that because your data
is in multiple locations

00:22:07.940 --> 00:22:09.560
throughout the
United States, it's

00:22:09.560 --> 00:22:15.510
more likely that any given user
will be closer to that data.

00:22:15.510 --> 00:22:17.160
On the right-hand
side then, you have

00:22:17.160 --> 00:22:19.110
Nearline and Coldline storage.

00:22:19.110 --> 00:22:23.940
And these are intended to
hold data at reduced costs,

00:22:23.940 --> 00:22:26.820
but it's data that you
don't access as often.

00:22:26.820 --> 00:22:29.460
So basically what happens is if
you put something in Nearline,

00:22:29.460 --> 00:22:31.530
Google essentially
puts it someplace

00:22:31.530 --> 00:22:34.050
that it's less expensive
for Google to store,

00:22:34.050 --> 00:22:36.660
but it's more expensive
for Google to serve.

00:22:36.660 --> 00:22:39.030
So what they do is they
cut the price for storage,

00:22:39.030 --> 00:22:41.520
but then they make you
pay for accessing it.

00:22:41.520 --> 00:22:43.200
You don't pay a
performance penalty,

00:22:43.200 --> 00:22:47.400
you still have access times
in tens of milliseconds

00:22:47.400 --> 00:22:49.650
and you still have
high throughput,

00:22:49.650 --> 00:22:52.080
but they have to go
through more contortions

00:22:52.080 --> 00:22:54.630
to serve the data at that speed.

00:22:54.630 --> 00:22:57.360
Therefore while it
costs less to store,

00:22:57.360 --> 00:22:59.280
it actually costs
more to access.

00:22:59.280 --> 00:23:03.630
So it's really good if you
access this data infrequently.

00:23:03.630 --> 00:23:07.620
So at the top here, we
show this is intended

00:23:07.620 --> 00:23:10.170
for data that is frequently
accessed across regions,

00:23:10.170 --> 00:23:12.780
within a region, accessed
less than once a month,

00:23:12.780 --> 00:23:14.820
or accessed less
than once a year.

00:23:14.820 --> 00:23:17.430
Durability is a lot of nines.

00:23:17.430 --> 00:23:22.020
Availability-- the worst-case
scenario is 1% of the time,

00:23:22.020 --> 00:23:26.100
you might not be able to access
your Nearline or Coldline

00:23:26.100 --> 00:23:28.770
data at a given point in time.

00:23:28.770 --> 00:23:30.510
And it's three
nines for regional

00:23:30.510 --> 00:23:34.590
and it's three nines and
a five for multi-regional.

00:23:34.590 --> 00:23:38.040
You use the same
APIs or the same GUI

00:23:38.040 --> 00:23:39.570
or the same command
line utilities

00:23:39.570 --> 00:23:42.730
for getting data in and out of
all of these storage classes.

00:23:42.730 --> 00:23:45.600
You have the same
latency for reads.

00:23:45.600 --> 00:23:47.760
The difference here is on cost.

00:23:47.760 --> 00:23:50.010
So at the top end--

00:23:50.010 --> 00:23:53.190
the top shelf multi-regional
storage class--

00:23:53.190 --> 00:23:58.620
you pay $0.026 per
gigabyte per month.

00:23:58.620 --> 00:24:01.320
For regional, you
get a discount.

00:24:01.320 --> 00:24:04.540
You pay $0.02 per
gigabyte per month.

00:24:04.540 --> 00:24:07.180
Nearline, you pay half that.

00:24:07.180 --> 00:24:10.084
You pay $0.01 per
gigabyte per month.

00:24:10.084 --> 00:24:11.500
What's interesting,
though, is you

00:24:11.500 --> 00:24:17.810
pay roughly $0.01 per gigabyte
to read the data off the disk.

00:24:17.810 --> 00:24:20.770
So if you read the data once
a month, you about break even.

00:24:20.770 --> 00:24:23.129
If you read the data
once every three months,

00:24:23.129 --> 00:24:25.420
you're clearly going to come
out ahead versus regional.

00:24:25.420 --> 00:24:27.128
If you read the data
three times a month,

00:24:27.128 --> 00:24:29.440
you would actually
pay more for Nearline

00:24:29.440 --> 00:24:31.090
than you did for regional.

00:24:31.090 --> 00:24:35.080
And then Coldline,
you pay 7/10 of a cent

00:24:35.080 --> 00:24:40.090
per gigabyte per month, but
you pay a higher access charge.

00:24:40.090 --> 00:24:44.020
Now, storage, when it
comes to the cost entailed

00:24:44.020 --> 00:24:47.620
in Google Cloud Storage, you
pay this ongoing storage fee.

00:24:47.620 --> 00:24:51.190
Then for Nearline and
Coldline, you pay to access--

00:24:51.190 --> 00:24:53.470
or read-- the data off the disk.

00:24:53.470 --> 00:24:57.400
And then you also pay for
what's called network egress.

00:24:57.400 --> 00:25:01.270
So that is any data that
leaves the region where

00:25:01.270 --> 00:25:06.750
the data is stored, you
end up having to pay.

00:25:06.750 --> 00:25:10.390
The lowest price is about
$0.08 per terabyte transfer.

00:25:10.390 --> 00:25:13.120
Now, again, if you're
using a regional bucket

00:25:13.120 --> 00:25:15.070
and your server is
in the West region

00:25:15.070 --> 00:25:17.350
and it's reading and writing
to a cloud bucket that's

00:25:17.350 --> 00:25:21.040
in the West region, it never
leaves the West region data

00:25:21.040 --> 00:25:24.640
centers, therefore you
don't pay network egress.

00:25:24.640 --> 00:25:27.370
But for instance, with that
learning management system

00:25:27.370 --> 00:25:30.030
with the little
video lesson, I'm

00:25:30.030 --> 00:25:35.730
paying $0.02 per gigabyte
per month to store it.

00:25:35.730 --> 00:25:38.970
And then every time somebody
loads that page, the data

00:25:38.970 --> 00:25:40.710
is leaving the data
center, and so I'm

00:25:40.710 --> 00:25:44.460
having to pay between
$0.08 and $0.11

00:25:44.460 --> 00:25:47.580
per terabyte of
storage transferred out

00:25:47.580 --> 00:25:49.450
of the data center.

00:25:49.450 --> 00:25:52.600
All in all, far less
expensive than building

00:25:52.600 --> 00:25:54.700
the same type of
infrastructure on premise--

00:25:54.700 --> 00:25:58.750
also far more secure,
far more reliable.

00:25:58.750 --> 00:26:03.400
Speaking of security,
you can rely on not only

00:26:03.400 --> 00:26:06.250
Google's hardware
infrastructure,

00:26:06.250 --> 00:26:10.060
but on their software and
processes, to secure your data.

00:26:10.060 --> 00:26:13.240
So Google has
invested huge amounts

00:26:13.240 --> 00:26:19.540
of money developing processes,
technical talent, and software

00:26:19.540 --> 00:26:20.920
that keeps its data secure.

00:26:20.920 --> 00:26:23.290
So they run at least a half
dozen applications that

00:26:23.290 --> 00:26:25.540
have a billion active users.

00:26:25.540 --> 00:26:27.280
Those need to be secure.

00:26:27.280 --> 00:26:29.740
And they're
basically leasing you

00:26:29.740 --> 00:26:31.930
space on their infrastructure.

00:26:31.930 --> 00:26:34.690
So for instance, all
your data is encrypted.

00:26:34.690 --> 00:26:37.000
Google automatically
encrypts the data.

00:26:37.000 --> 00:26:39.580
They hold the encryption keys
and rotate those encryption

00:26:39.580 --> 00:26:42.340
keys on a well-documented basis.

00:26:42.340 --> 00:26:45.760
If you work in an industry where
you have to manage the keys,

00:26:45.760 --> 00:26:48.580
you can use GCS with
customer-supplied encryption

00:26:48.580 --> 00:26:50.930
keys.

00:26:50.930 --> 00:26:53.250
IAM is Identity and
Access Management.

00:26:53.250 --> 00:26:56.090
This is a mechanism
that you use to control

00:26:56.090 --> 00:27:00.530
who can do what to which
resources in your GCS projects.

00:27:00.530 --> 00:27:04.610
And the same permissioning
scheme and authentication

00:27:04.610 --> 00:27:08.060
system that you use to manage
access to Compute Engine

00:27:08.060 --> 00:27:10.370
instances or to
BigQuery tables, you

00:27:10.370 --> 00:27:14.030
can use to control access
to buckets and objects

00:27:14.030 --> 00:27:15.900
within your project.

00:27:15.900 --> 00:27:18.930
In addition to that--
so, for instance,

00:27:18.930 --> 00:27:21.270
editors on your project
can read and write

00:27:21.270 --> 00:27:23.320
to all of your
buckets by default.

00:27:23.320 --> 00:27:26.370
But let's say you have a
particular bucket that you

00:27:26.370 --> 00:27:29.580
don't want every editor on
your project to have access to,

00:27:29.580 --> 00:27:33.870
only a very few particular
people should have access.

00:27:33.870 --> 00:27:35.650
You have an additional
layer of security,

00:27:35.650 --> 00:27:37.410
which is access control lists.

00:27:37.410 --> 00:27:40.350
And those access control lists
can be set at the bucket level

00:27:40.350 --> 00:27:42.480
and you can have
default permissions set

00:27:42.480 --> 00:27:45.480
for every object inside, or
you can manually and explicitly

00:27:45.480 --> 00:27:49.540
set access control lists
on individual objects.

00:27:49.540 --> 00:27:52.750
In some cases, you want
to make access to objects

00:27:52.750 --> 00:27:54.400
within a bucket
available to people

00:27:54.400 --> 00:27:56.200
who don't have Google accounts.

00:27:56.200 --> 00:28:00.610
So one thing about IAM
is it requires the person

00:28:00.610 --> 00:28:05.120
to whom you're granting roles
to have a Google account.

00:28:05.120 --> 00:28:08.092
In a lot of cases, you
are serving up media

00:28:08.092 --> 00:28:10.550
and you may want to serve that
to the public, in which case

00:28:10.550 --> 00:28:13.406
you set a special ACL that says
it's available to everyone.

00:28:13.406 --> 00:28:14.780
But in other cases
you don't want

00:28:14.780 --> 00:28:16.610
it to be publicly
available, you want

00:28:16.610 --> 00:28:18.530
to secure it for
particular individuals

00:28:18.530 --> 00:28:21.860
but you can't guarantee
that those individuals have

00:28:21.860 --> 00:28:23.190
Google accounts.

00:28:23.190 --> 00:28:25.820
So there are code libraries
available for developers

00:28:25.820 --> 00:28:27.770
to create what are
called Signed URLs.

00:28:27.770 --> 00:28:32.270
And these URLs basically
have an indication

00:28:32.270 --> 00:28:38.060
of what a person can do to
a given resource in GCS,

00:28:38.060 --> 00:28:40.640
how long that
permission is granted.

00:28:40.640 --> 00:28:44.270
And then anybody who holds
that URL can actually

00:28:44.270 --> 00:28:47.000
access without having
a Google account.

00:28:47.000 --> 00:28:49.010
So this is not
unlike those password

00:28:49.010 --> 00:28:51.921
reset verification emails,
where it goes to your email

00:28:51.921 --> 00:28:53.670
and it gives you a big
long arbitrary link

00:28:53.670 --> 00:28:56.446
and if you click on that then
you can reset your password.

00:28:56.446 --> 00:28:58.070
And having that link
is your permission

00:28:58.070 --> 00:29:00.320
to reset your password and it's
only good for a certain period

00:29:00.320 --> 00:29:00.920
of time.

00:29:00.920 --> 00:29:03.860
This is similar in concept.

00:29:03.860 --> 00:29:07.010
And for those of you who develop
mobile applications using

00:29:07.010 --> 00:29:09.740
the Firebase mobile
development framework,

00:29:09.740 --> 00:29:13.430
Firebase actually has their own
storage security rules system

00:29:13.430 --> 00:29:15.920
which makes it really
easy for developers

00:29:15.920 --> 00:29:18.530
to create security rules
without having to worry

00:29:18.530 --> 00:29:20.000
about the particulars of GCS.

00:29:20.000 --> 00:29:26.050
And they apply directly
to GCS on your behalf.

00:29:26.050 --> 00:29:29.920
Ways to get data in to
Google Cloud Storage.

00:29:29.920 --> 00:29:32.690
So there are simple ways,
there are interactive ways.

00:29:32.690 --> 00:29:36.280
So you can go through the GUI
and click a button and upload

00:29:36.280 --> 00:29:37.610
a file.

00:29:37.610 --> 00:29:40.600
You can use the
gsutil utility to copy

00:29:40.600 --> 00:29:43.540
a file from your local machine.

00:29:43.540 --> 00:29:46.570
But obviously if you're doing
large quantities of files

00:29:46.570 --> 00:29:50.320
or maybe really 10
100-terabyte files,

00:29:50.320 --> 00:29:53.140
doing sort of an interactive
session from your local machine

00:29:53.140 --> 00:29:55.600
probably doesn't
make a lot of sense.

00:29:55.600 --> 00:29:58.030
Other ways to get data
in include integrations

00:29:58.030 --> 00:29:59.219
with other GCP services.

00:29:59.219 --> 00:30:01.510
So for instance, I showed
you the BigQuery integration.

00:30:01.510 --> 00:30:04.000
You can just as easily
dump a huge BigQuery

00:30:04.000 --> 00:30:07.660
table or the results
of a big data process,

00:30:07.660 --> 00:30:11.740
you can dump that all out
into Google Cloud Storage

00:30:11.740 --> 00:30:13.162
very easily.

00:30:13.162 --> 00:30:14.620
There are third-party
integrations,

00:30:14.620 --> 00:30:16.330
and the Retrospect
software that I showed

00:30:16.330 --> 00:30:17.710
you was an example of that.

00:30:17.710 --> 00:30:21.340
That is third-party backup
software that writes data

00:30:21.340 --> 00:30:23.830
directly into the Cloud.

00:30:23.830 --> 00:30:26.800
For really large
volumes of data,

00:30:26.800 --> 00:30:29.470
you might go with a
third-party service

00:30:29.470 --> 00:30:31.480
or a third-party appliance.

00:30:31.480 --> 00:30:34.330
So companies like
Zadara basically

00:30:34.330 --> 00:30:37.390
create appliances
that they put on-prem

00:30:37.390 --> 00:30:38.890
and they act as
bridges, where they

00:30:38.890 --> 00:30:41.440
pull huge amounts
of on-prem data

00:30:41.440 --> 00:30:44.800
and they pump them through very
high-speed network connections

00:30:44.800 --> 00:30:46.270
up into the Cloud.

00:30:46.270 --> 00:30:47.755
And then third-party
services would

00:30:47.755 --> 00:30:50.380
be something like Iron Mountain,
who will actually come to you,

00:30:50.380 --> 00:30:52.660
pick up a bunch of your
media, take it offsite,

00:30:52.660 --> 00:30:56.170
and upload it to
the Cloud for you.

00:30:56.170 --> 00:30:58.827
Getting data out-- sort
of the reverse process.

00:30:58.827 --> 00:31:00.410
So again, you can
do it interactively.

00:31:00.410 --> 00:31:02.830
So you can just click on a
link and download a file.

00:31:02.830 --> 00:31:06.190
You can use gsutil to copy
files out of the Cloud

00:31:06.190 --> 00:31:08.140
onto an on-prem device.

00:31:08.140 --> 00:31:12.100
Again, data can go out of
GCS into other Google Cloud

00:31:12.100 --> 00:31:13.930
Platform services very easily.

00:31:13.930 --> 00:31:16.450
We showed that with BigQuery.

00:31:16.450 --> 00:31:19.460
The LMS system
that I showed you,

00:31:19.460 --> 00:31:22.610
where you had that little
video module about mantas,

00:31:22.610 --> 00:31:24.420
that's using proxy web access.

00:31:24.420 --> 00:31:26.780
So basically you hit a web
server, and the web server--

00:31:26.780 --> 00:31:30.620
when it sees a URL pointing to
a Google Cloud Storage URL--

00:31:30.620 --> 00:31:34.010
it will proxy-- it'll
grab the data out of GCS--

00:31:34.010 --> 00:31:35.000
and then serve it up.

00:31:35.000 --> 00:31:38.340
Or you can even serve
files directly out of GCS.

00:31:38.340 --> 00:31:40.580
So I showed you where
the public links are.

00:31:40.580 --> 00:31:43.372
If you click on that link, GCS
actually acts as a web server,

00:31:43.372 --> 00:31:45.080
so you can upload a
bunch of static files

00:31:45.080 --> 00:31:47.360
and run a web server
out of GCS without

00:31:47.360 --> 00:31:51.010
any additional software.

00:31:51.010 --> 00:31:52.220
To manage your data--

00:31:52.220 --> 00:31:54.520
I've already highlighted
this a couple of times--

00:31:54.520 --> 00:31:57.010
you have a consistent
set of APIs

00:31:57.010 --> 00:32:00.040
that you can use across
all the storage classes.

00:32:00.040 --> 00:32:01.720
There are also some
really cool features

00:32:01.720 --> 00:32:05.080
that make it easier to keep
just the right amount of data

00:32:05.080 --> 00:32:06.620
and just the right
versions of data.

00:32:06.620 --> 00:32:08.830
So for instance, there's
Lifecycle Management.

00:32:08.830 --> 00:32:12.130
What this allows you to do
is say, hey, any files that

00:32:12.130 --> 00:32:16.420
are older than 365 days, I
want to move them into Nearline

00:32:16.420 --> 00:32:17.120
from regional.

00:32:17.120 --> 00:32:19.744
So I actually want to move them
into a different storage class,

00:32:19.744 --> 00:32:21.940
and therefore I
get to save money.

00:32:21.940 --> 00:32:24.580
Or I want to only
keep the latest

00:32:24.580 --> 00:32:26.680
three versions of
a particular file

00:32:26.680 --> 00:32:29.080
and throw away anything
older than that.

00:32:29.080 --> 00:32:31.450
So you set these as
little JSON snippets.

00:32:31.450 --> 00:32:35.836
They describe what a particular
bucket or object does,

00:32:35.836 --> 00:32:37.210
in terms of
lifecycle management,

00:32:37.210 --> 00:32:39.820
and then the system
takes care of it for you.

00:32:39.820 --> 00:32:42.760
Speaking of versioning, there
is object versioning within GCS.

00:32:42.760 --> 00:32:45.280
And what this allows you to
do is basically say, when

00:32:45.280 --> 00:32:48.490
you overwrite an existing
object with a new object,

00:32:48.490 --> 00:32:50.950
it keeps the previous version.

00:32:50.950 --> 00:32:54.010
And so you can override it
over and over and over again

00:32:54.010 --> 00:32:57.100
and have lots of different
versions of the same object.

00:32:57.100 --> 00:32:59.620
And then at any time, you
can list those versions

00:32:59.620 --> 00:33:01.786
and you can actually
recover a version that

00:33:01.786 --> 00:33:02.910
has been previously stored.

00:33:02.910 --> 00:33:04.870
Now, obviously, you
have to pay for all

00:33:04.870 --> 00:33:06.700
these multiple versions,
so that stacks up.

00:33:06.700 --> 00:33:09.130
And that's why you combine
it with Lifecycle Management

00:33:09.130 --> 00:33:12.900
to maybe age out and only
keep the last three versions.

00:33:12.900 --> 00:33:15.130
An object change
notification is a feature

00:33:15.130 --> 00:33:19.270
where you can have a bucket
notify an external application

00:33:19.270 --> 00:33:22.570
every time a new object gets
dropped into the bucket,

00:33:22.570 --> 00:33:24.490
for instance.

00:33:24.490 --> 00:33:28.030
So let's say you had an
application where users

00:33:28.030 --> 00:33:30.520
uploaded photos, and every
time a photo got uploaded,

00:33:30.520 --> 00:33:32.860
you wanted to actually
create a thumbnail.

00:33:32.860 --> 00:33:35.800
So you wanted a service
to open the image,

00:33:35.800 --> 00:33:39.460
shrink it down, and save
the thumbnail in a database,

00:33:39.460 --> 00:33:40.481
for instance.

00:33:40.481 --> 00:33:42.730
What you would do is you
would configure object change

00:33:42.730 --> 00:33:44.020
notification on the bucket.

00:33:44.020 --> 00:33:48.820
Every time a new file gets
uploaded, a webhook fires,

00:33:48.820 --> 00:33:50.760
talks to your
server application,

00:33:50.760 --> 00:33:54.930
that then responds by
creating the thumbnail.

00:33:54.930 --> 00:33:57.300
So given all these
capabilities, you

00:33:57.300 --> 00:33:58.830
want to start
thinking about, where

00:33:58.830 --> 00:34:01.710
can we leverage Cloud
Storage to solve

00:34:01.710 --> 00:34:03.840
problems for our business?

00:34:03.840 --> 00:34:07.320
One example here is the
Autism Speaks project.

00:34:07.320 --> 00:34:09.090
So what Autism
Speaks wanted to do

00:34:09.090 --> 00:34:12.960
was create a really
large shared dataset

00:34:12.960 --> 00:34:16.330
about genomic information
related to autism.

00:34:16.330 --> 00:34:22.730
So every sequenced genome takes
up to 200 gigabytes of data.

00:34:22.730 --> 00:34:27.159
They started by sequencing
roughly 1,300 genomes,

00:34:27.159 --> 00:34:31.420
so this created over
100 terabytes of data.

00:34:31.420 --> 00:34:34.900
Now, 100 terabytes of data
doesn't seem that large,

00:34:34.900 --> 00:34:38.260
but, remember, the target
wasn't just these 1,300,

00:34:38.260 --> 00:34:42.190
they wanted to have
10,000 plus people who

00:34:42.190 --> 00:34:43.600
had their genomes sequenced.

00:34:43.600 --> 00:34:46.870
And now you're talking
multiple petabytes of data.

00:34:46.870 --> 00:34:48.940
That's a significant
amount of data.

00:34:48.940 --> 00:34:51.850
Where can you store
this data so that you

00:34:51.850 --> 00:34:54.370
can control who
has access to it,

00:34:54.370 --> 00:34:57.850
you can host it
relatively inexpensively,

00:34:57.850 --> 00:35:01.480
and it has high-performance
downloads around the world?

00:35:01.480 --> 00:35:05.590
Well, they did it on Google
Cloud Platform using GCS.

00:35:05.590 --> 00:35:07.840
So they ended up creating
this dataset that

00:35:07.840 --> 00:35:11.050
could be used by the best
scientists in the world

00:35:11.050 --> 00:35:14.710
to run analyses against
trillions of data points

00:35:14.710 --> 00:35:19.210
to try to find new ways
of addressing autism

00:35:19.210 --> 00:35:21.600
in individuals.

00:35:21.600 --> 00:35:23.520
You may not have
something quite this big

00:35:23.520 --> 00:35:25.269
or you may have something
actually bigger.

00:35:25.269 --> 00:35:28.960
In any case, GCS probably
will work really well for you.

00:35:28.960 --> 00:35:31.210
Now, after this session, if
you come up with questions

00:35:31.210 --> 00:35:33.910
and you want to learn
more about Cloud Storage,

00:35:33.910 --> 00:35:35.090
where do you look?

00:35:35.090 --> 00:35:37.180
Search is your friend.

00:35:37.180 --> 00:35:39.505
If you search on
Google Cloud Storage

00:35:39.505 --> 00:35:41.380
and then whatever topic
you're interested in,

00:35:41.380 --> 00:35:42.880
it's very likely
that the first link

00:35:42.880 --> 00:35:44.088
is going to be what you want.

00:35:44.088 --> 00:35:46.270
So for example, here we've
searched on Google Cloud

00:35:46.270 --> 00:35:48.250
Storage classes
and what we get is

00:35:48.250 --> 00:35:50.590
a link to the article that
has all the information that

00:35:50.590 --> 00:35:53.720
fed the table that
we looked at earlier.

00:35:53.720 --> 00:35:56.440
If you're trying to figure
out the right command line

00:35:56.440 --> 00:36:01.010
structure for doing things
using the gsutil utility,

00:36:01.010 --> 00:36:02.789
type in gsutil and
then the action

00:36:02.789 --> 00:36:03.830
that you want to perform.

00:36:03.830 --> 00:36:10.900
So like gsutil copy or
gsutil move or gsutil rename.

00:36:10.900 --> 00:36:15.100
Again, like every
other GCP project,

00:36:15.100 --> 00:36:18.550
the Google Cloud Storage
documentation at the top level

00:36:18.550 --> 00:36:21.100
has got this nice six sections.

00:36:21.100 --> 00:36:24.070
It's got a quick start, it's
got a series of how-to guides,

00:36:24.070 --> 00:36:27.250
such as how to create buckets,
how to upload or download

00:36:27.250 --> 00:36:30.130
files, how to
create signed URLs.

00:36:30.130 --> 00:36:33.940
Then it has deep dives into
the APIs and general concepts.

00:36:33.940 --> 00:36:36.110
It has tutorials
and other resources,

00:36:36.110 --> 00:36:39.130
including the pricing
information and link

00:36:39.130 --> 00:36:42.010
to a pricing calculator,
which you can use to predict

00:36:42.010 --> 00:36:45.190
what your costs will be.

00:36:45.190 --> 00:36:47.800
Some things to
consider as you're

00:36:47.800 --> 00:36:52.750
evaluating applying Google Cloud
Storage to particular use cases

00:36:52.750 --> 00:36:54.220
in your organization.

00:36:54.220 --> 00:36:58.220
Again, have a process
and be methodical.

00:36:58.220 --> 00:37:01.000
It's not hard, but
do a good job of it.

00:37:01.000 --> 00:37:04.180
Figure out what it is that
you need to know in order

00:37:04.180 --> 00:37:08.170
to determine if GCS is
appropriate for the use cases

00:37:08.170 --> 00:37:09.130
you're thinking about.

00:37:09.130 --> 00:37:11.590
Figure out what you
need to test and what

00:37:11.590 --> 00:37:14.770
your acceptance threshold is.

00:37:14.770 --> 00:37:17.800
So for instance, we need
to know that we get x kind

00:37:17.800 --> 00:37:19.840
of performance in
Australia or we

00:37:19.840 --> 00:37:22.330
need to know that we can
keep our monthly budget

00:37:22.330 --> 00:37:24.250
below a certain level.

00:37:24.250 --> 00:37:26.740
Then figure out how you're
going to do the testing.

00:37:26.740 --> 00:37:30.460
So you need to figure out how
many buckets, how many objects,

00:37:30.460 --> 00:37:32.624
how much data, what
kind of integrations

00:37:32.624 --> 00:37:34.540
you're going to use with
third-party products,

00:37:34.540 --> 00:37:35.470
and so forth.

00:37:35.470 --> 00:37:38.162
And do a little bit of
research to figure out how

00:37:38.162 --> 00:37:39.370
you're going to implement it.

00:37:39.370 --> 00:37:41.328
And then figure out who's
going to participate.

00:37:41.328 --> 00:37:43.737
Somebody probably is going
to architect the solution,

00:37:43.737 --> 00:37:45.820
somebody is going to be
responsible for setting it

00:37:45.820 --> 00:37:47.620
up and operating it.

00:37:47.620 --> 00:37:50.110
And then somebody is project
managing the whole process,

00:37:50.110 --> 00:37:52.141
collecting the results,
analyzing the results.

00:37:52.141 --> 00:37:53.890
And then, obviously,
your business leaders

00:37:53.890 --> 00:37:56.740
will be making a decision.

00:37:56.740 --> 00:37:59.440
Again, timelines,
you can typically

00:37:59.440 --> 00:38:01.870
do one use case within a month.

00:38:01.870 --> 00:38:03.430
Often it can be
faster than that,

00:38:03.430 --> 00:38:07.240
although it depends on how
complex the solution is.

00:38:07.240 --> 00:38:10.090
Typically, you break this
down into one segment

00:38:10.090 --> 00:38:12.730
that's about defining your
criteria, recruiting your team,

00:38:12.730 --> 00:38:14.620
and agreeing on
what you're testing.

00:38:14.620 --> 00:38:16.510
A second segment,
which is figuring out

00:38:16.510 --> 00:38:17.920
how you're going to test--

00:38:17.920 --> 00:38:21.040
this is generally some
point testing and some trial

00:38:21.040 --> 00:38:22.840
and error and some research.

00:38:22.840 --> 00:38:24.850
And then, ultimately,
you perform your tests,

00:38:24.850 --> 00:38:28.930
you collect your data, and
you analyze the results

00:38:28.930 --> 00:38:31.720
and you make a decision.

00:38:31.720 --> 00:38:33.350
If you put more
people against this,

00:38:33.350 --> 00:38:35.164
you can get more
use cases tested out

00:38:35.164 --> 00:38:36.080
in the same time line.

00:38:36.080 --> 00:38:37.990
If it's a really
sophisticated solution,

00:38:37.990 --> 00:38:40.180
it may take you a
little bit longer.

00:38:40.180 --> 00:38:42.640
But hopefully this
gives you a sense

00:38:42.640 --> 00:38:46.390
of how to tackle the
evaluation of GCS

00:38:46.390 --> 00:38:51.400
as an appropriate technology
for your use cases.

00:38:51.400 --> 00:38:53.140
And that's it for this session.

00:38:53.140 --> 00:38:54.590
Thanks so much for joining us.

00:38:54.590 --> 00:38:57.010
I hope you learned something
and that you enjoyed it.

00:38:57.010 --> 00:38:58.780
We'll be sending you
a followup email that

00:38:58.780 --> 00:39:01.279
has a link to a code lab, where
you can get your hands dirty

00:39:01.279 --> 00:39:03.850
and try GCS out for yourself.

00:39:03.850 --> 00:39:06.970
We'd love your feedback, so make
sure to provide that on screen.

00:39:06.970 --> 00:39:09.335
And stay tuned for
our next session.

